# 11. What I Would Test First

If I have limited time, I would not start with minor UI issues. I would first test the functionality that can affect user data.

My order would be:

**1. Registration → 2. Login → 3. Authentication/Authorization → 4. Create Task → 5. View Task → 6. Edit Task → 7. Delete Task → 8. Validation → 9. Error Handling → 10. Regression Testing**

I would especially test whether users can access another user's data because that would be a production-blocking issue.

---

# 12. Final Testing Thought

For me, successful testing does not only mean checking whether the buttons work. I would also check what happens to the data after every operation.

For example, if I create a task and it appears on the screen, I would not consider the test completely successful yet. I would refresh the page or log in again and check whether the same task is still available. Similarly, after editing or deleting a task, I would verify that the change is actually reflected in the stored data.

I would also test the application from the point of view of different users to make sure that one user's data cannot be accessed or modified by another user.

Since this application is close to production, I would recommend fixing all **Critical** issues before release and giving high priority to issues related to authentication, authorization, database consistency and core CRUD functionality.
